    "message": {
      "content": "# Validation Checklist for Multi-Node Language-Model Serving Systems\n\n## Purpose and Scope\n\nThis checklist validates that a distributed LLM serving system (tensor-parallel, pipeline-parallel, expert-parallel, or data-parallel deployments) behaves correctly under load, fails predictably, and produces trustworthy performance measurements. It assumes a cluster of N nodes, each running one or more serving processes (\"ranks\"), coordinated by a scheduler or orchestrator.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Configuration Identity\n\n**Goal:** Prove that every rank is running the same logical system before any measurement is trusted.\n\n- [ ] **Model artifact hash.** Record SHA-256 of model weights, tokenizer files, and config files. Verify all ranks load byte-identical artifacts. For sharded weights, hash each shard and verify shard-to-rank assignment matches the parallelism plan.\n- [ ] **Code and container version.** Record image digests (not just tags), commit SHAs, and dependency lock files. Verify no rank runs a stale image from a previous rollout.\n- [ ] **Parallelism topology.** Confirm tensor-parallel size, pipeline-parallel size, expert-parallel size, and data-parallel replicas match the intended plan on every rank. Verify `world_size` and local rank assignments as seen by the framework agree with the orchestrator's view.\n- [ ] **Serving configuration parity.** Diff the effective runtime config (max batch size, max sequence length, KV-cache budget, quantization settings, dtype, attention backend, sampling defaults, temperature/top-p handling) across all ranks. Log the *effective* config, not just the file \u2014 defaults may differ across versions.\n- [ ] **Environment parity.** Verify CUDA/driver versions, NCCL version and topology detection, network fabric settings (e.g., InfiniBand vs. Ethernet fallback), and CPU pinning are consistent. A silent IB-to-TCP fallback is a classic hidden performance regression.\n- [ ]",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
